# Reviewing AI-generated code

Generation is the cheap part. This list is the expensive part, and it is the
reason agent-assisted work in this repo is safe to merge.

Run through it on your own output before reporting a task as done.

## 1. Correctness of the money path

- [ ] Any price is `Money` (integer minor units), not a `number`
- [ ] No `toFixed()`, `parseFloat()` or `/ 100` inside a calculation
- [ ] Totals are derived by `totals()` in `@fc/core`, not recomputed inline
- [ ] The server re-derives prices from the catalogue; nothing trusts the client

## 2. Trust boundaries

- [ ] Every request body / query string / external response is Zod-parsed
- [ ] No `as` on untrusted data
- [ ] New route handler calls `requireUser()` unless it is genuinely public
- [ ] An order-creating mutation is idempotent on a key
- [ ] Nothing new is read from or written to `localStorage` without validation

## 3. Leaks

- [ ] No token, secret, raw body or stack trace in a log or a response
- [ ] No `console.log` left behind
- [ ] Error messages shown to users say what to do, not what broke internally
- [ ] No hardcoded credential, endpoint or key — config comes from env

## 4. Layering

- [ ] A rule that would also be true on mobile lives in `@fc/core`
- [ ] `@fc/ui` gained no routing, fetching or business logic
- [ ] `@fc/core` gained no React / Next / Node import
- [ ] No new barrel file, no deep import into another package's `src`

## 5. React and Next specifics

- [ ] `'use client'` only where genuinely needed, and as low in the tree as possible
- [ ] No `cookies()` / `headers()` added to the root layout
- [ ] `npm run build` still shows `○`/`●` for the catalogue routes
- [ ] No Zustand selector that constructs a new object
- [ ] Effects have correct dependencies and no `setState` in the body
- [ ] Keys on lists are stable ids, not array indices

## 6. Accessibility

- [ ] Interactive elements have accessible names and keyboard support
- [ ] Errors are wired with `aria-describedby` + `role="alert"`
- [ ] Images have meaningful `alt`, or `alt=""` when decorative
- [ ] Nothing relies on colour alone
- [ ] Still usable at 320px

## 7. Tests

- [ ] New behaviour has a test at the cheapest level that catches it
- [ ] A bug fix has a test that failed before the fix
- [ ] No assertion was weakened to get to green
- [ ] No snapshot, no class-name assertion, no `waitForTimeout`

## 8. Scope

- [ ] Nothing was refactored that the task did not require
- [ ] No new dependency, or a stated reason for it
- [ ] No API surface invented "for later"

## 9. Honesty of the report

- [ ] `npm run verify` actually ran and actually passed
- [ ] Anything untested, partial or guessed is named explicitly
- [ ] Assumptions made are listed, not buried

The failure mode this list exists to prevent is not ugly code. It is plausible
code: correct-looking, well-formatted, and wrong in a way that only shows up in
an order total.
